[IDSP.14.2] The table is the entire boundary between compiled RHS code and
the engine. Six VERB entry points, and nothing else a blob can effect: assert into
working memory, publish, call a service, set a cache entry, delete one, log.
Slot 5 is reserved: it holds no entry and its number is never reissued
([STYL.2.8.7]). The membership of that list is therefore a **capability
list** -- anything outside it is not merely unimplemented for compiled code,
it is unreachable from it.

**Slot 7 is not a verb, and the table is one entry longer than the capability
list.** `fmt_float` is a formatting service the engine lends to compiled code:
the freestanding AOT `.text` carries no libc, so the generated C routes every
float render through it. It performs no effect, reaches no registry and
is observable only in a string the caller already owns, so it is inside the ABI
and outside the capability list. The two counts differ by exactly this entry and
that is deliberate — a blob can *format* without being able to *do* anything.

Two properties keep the boundary stable. Every entry takes the engine as its
first argument and reports through the universal result type, so a blob holds
no state of its own. And the things it names -- a schema, a cache key, a
service -- cross as **names** rather than as resolved handles, so a blob
carries no pointer into a registry and needs to know nothing about how one is
laid out.

The slot numbering is not commentary. It is the contract with the slot table
at the front of the blob itself ([IDSP.14.1], [IDSP.14.3]).

```c
typedef struct ds_dispatch_t {
    /* Slot 0 */ ds_result_t (*wm_assert)      (struct ds_engine *,
                                 const char *schema_name,
                                 ds_rete_val_t *slots,
                                 uint32_t slot_count,
                                 bool is_logical,
                                 ds_rete_token_t *justifier);
    /* Slot 1 */ ds_result_t (*publish)        (struct ds_engine *,
                                 const char *schema_name,
                                 ds_rete_val_t *slots,
                                 uint32_t slot_count);
    /* Slot 2 */ ds_result_t (*call)           (struct ds_engine *,
                                 const char *service,
                                 const char *correlation,
                                 const ds_arg_t *args,
                                 uint32_t    arg_count,
                                 uint64_t    cooldown_ns);
    /* Slot 3 */ ds_result_t (*cache_set)      (struct ds_engine *,
                                 const char *key,
                                 const char *value_json,
                                 int64_t     ttl_ns);
    /* Slot 4 */ ds_result_t (*cache_delete)   (struct ds_engine *,
                                 const char *key);
    /* Slot 5 */ void        *reserved5;      /* reserved — no entry */
    /* Slot 6 */ ds_result_t (*log)            (struct ds_engine *,
                                 ds_log_level_t  level,
                                 const char     *rule_name,
                                 const char     *message);
    /* Slot 7 */ void        (*fmt_float)      (char *buf, size_t cap,
                                 double v);   /* NOT a verb — see above */
} ds_dispatch_t;
```
